never queue, never retry | `RejectedByServer` – \"Server refused the sketch (4xx).\" |\n\nNo new `PublishOutcome` values are needed; `StatusCode` + `Message` distinguish the cases.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. `unity/.../Runtime/Net/PublishService.cs` (main change)\n\n- Add tunables as public properties so tests can zero them: `MaxAttempts = 3`, `RetryDelaySeconds = 1f` (doubles per attempt), `AutoDrain = true`, `DrainIntervalSeconds = 15f`.\n- Extract `private IEnumerator SendRoutine(FieldSketchDto sketch, Action<PublishResult> onDone)`:\n  stamps `clientLengthFeet` / `clientVersion`, serialises, runs the attempt loop (`WaitForSecondsRealtime` between transient retries), classifies per the table and returns a `PublishResult`. It does **not** touch `IsBusy` or the queue, so publish and drain can share it.\n- `PublishRoutine`: validate locally (unchanged) -> `SendRoutine` -> apply the queue action:\n  - offline / transient-exhausted: enqueue a **copy** of the sketch (`JsonUtility` round-trip) then `Queue.Save()`. The copy matters: `controller.Current` stays editable after publish, and resending an edited sketch under an id that may already have landed would turn a safe retry into a `409`.\n  - success: `Queue.Remove(id)` + `Save()` if it was pending, then kick a drain (signal is evidently back).\n- Implement `DrainQueue()`:\n  - no-op if `IsBusy` or queue empty; sets `IsBusy` for the whole drain so the HUD's existing `IsBusy` guard keeps a manual publish from interleaving.\n  - loop on `Queue.Peek()`: success -> `Remove` + `Save`, continue; permanent rejection -> `Remove` + `Save`, raise `PublishCompleted` with the rejection so the foreman sees it, continue (otherwise one bad sketch blocks the queue forever); offline/transient -> stop, leave it and everything behind it queued.\n  - finish with one summary result (\"Sent N queued sketches.\" / \"Still offline, N pending.\").\n- Triggers for the drain: `Start()` (pending work from a previous session), after any successful p